{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_001's core claim that jitter in implementation and deployment prevents 'perfect correlation' conflates operational timing variation with fault-independence. The problem asks what evidence distinguishes truly independent failure modes from merely uncorrelated-appearing ones, and SRC_001's own disanalogy admits there is 'no central system integrator to ensure the variants are actually independent.' This means the 'stochastic variation' it relies on is precisely the kind of independence-by-appearance that the question warns may mask a shared cause.","counterexample":[{"scenario":"1000 independent operators all deploy the same patched TLS library version within a 48-hour jitter window; failures appear uncorrelated across time but share the identical root cause, demonstrating jitter in deployment timing does not establish failure independence."}],"target":"39e163afc808e3e1037da9fa233cd2e0a9190127fb64ad3f25b17c2b66c9edbb"},{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_002 invokes 'self-organized criticality' but never explains what concrete evidence would distinguish this from a simpler shared-cause correlation. The problem explicitly asks to identify evidence that separates truly independent failures from merely uncorrelated-appearing ones; SRC_002 argues the network synchronizes on external signals but its counterconditions (heterogeneous monitoring models, absence of external signals) are restatements of the hypothesis, not operational evidence tests that would adjudicate whether observed correlation is self-organized or driven by a single shared cause.","counterexample":[{"scenario":"An operator network appears to fail in synchronized waves consistent with SOC, but analysis reveals all operators consumed the same upstream DNS poisoning feed; without an evidence criterion, SRC_002's framework cannot distinguish this shared-cause failure from emergent self-organized synchronization."}],"target":"68ff86db3bfd567655a4bd6f31fb73f2ca4ef42a3f5dc1f4802649a8e